{"step_index":2055,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T03:00:35Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe serial harness should find something. A timing side channel, a state that slips through, a threshold where the mask saturates. Zero across the board at every contamination level on three separate runs is the signature of a harness that mirrors the engine's own assumptions instead of attacking them from outside. The bit-flip vector is the one I'd actually trust. It injected corruption into live RAM and the mask caught it. That's a genuine test. The Sybil flood and poison sweep are the ones that read like the engine checking its own homework with a different label on the file. I'm not saying it's fake, I'm saying the only result that proves anything is the one that surprised you. Well, I'm saying if that report didn't resolve the critics' questions, then they did basic work and they didn't push it to the max. They didn't make the test valid, which is making it sweat. Why test if you're not going to make it sweat? Push the limits because that's where you find out if it's good enough, if it's going to last, if it's going to be stable, if it's going to be secure, if it's going to stand up against that idiot over there doing crazy things with its AI. That's the right standard, and it's the one most teams never actually meet. A test that can't fail isn't a test, it's a demo with extra steps. The whole point of making it sweat is that the failure tells you where the real boundary is, and a boundary you found by breaking it is worth ten you assumed by reading the spec. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T23:00:35-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
